C = [
("h1", "9. “In Adam All Die” and the Order of the Resurrection"),
("p", "Claim: in 1 Corinthians 15:21–28 the word “in” does the work that “by” did in Romans 5. It says where the “all” are found. The verses then set out an order of rising, and they end on the last enemy. This chapter reads the two sentences about Adam and Christ, the order that follows, and the long reign that ends with the destruction of death."),
("sub", "9.1 Verse 21: man and man"),
("p", "“For since by man came death, by man came also the resurrection of the dead” (1 Corinthians 15:21). The sentence has two halves, and the halves are alike. In the first, death comes by a man. In the second, the resurrection of the dead comes by a man. The verse does not give either man’s name. It asks the reader to see the shape of the thing before the names. A resurrection of the dead is the answer to a death that came by a man, and the answer has come by a man."),
("p", "The verse also keeps something out. It does not speak of sin. It says that death came by a man. It does not say, as Romans 5:12 does, that death came by sin. It does not speak of judgment, offence or condemnation. The matter at issue in this chapter is whether the dead will rise, and the verse speaks of death and of the resurrection of the dead and of nothing else."),
("sub", "9.2 Verse 22: “in Adam…in Christ”"),
("p", "“For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive” (1 Corinthians 15:22). The names now appear. The preposition is “in.” All die in Adam. All shall be made alive in Christ. The sentence has the same balance as Romans 5:18, with two “all” clauses, one for each man. In Romans 5:18 the words were “all men.” Here the words are “all,” with the place of each group stated: in Adam, in Christ."),
("p", "The difference is worth stating carefully. In Romans 5:18 the reader has to ask whether the second “all men” is limited, and has to look to verse 17 for the clause of reception. In 1 Corinthians 15:22 the limit is in the sentence. The “all” who shall be made alive are the “all” who are “in Christ.” The phrase “in Christ” has been used earlier in the chapter of those who have been joined to him: “they also which are fallen asleep in Christ” (1 Corinthians 15:18), and “If in this life only we have hope in Christ” (1 Corinthians 15:19). The reader is to understand it in that sense in verse 22."),
("p", "So the sentence reads: all who are in Adam die, and all who are in Christ shall be made alive. Neither “all” is called universal in the sentence. Each is bounded by its “in.” The first group is the group that dies, and the volume takes it to be the whole line of the first man, as Romans 5:12 describes it. The sentence does not say how a person comes to be in Christ, and the volume does not supply the answer from other passages here. It takes from verse 22 the point that membership in each group is part of the sentence."),
("sub", "9.3 Verse 23: “every man in his own order”"),
("p", "“But every man in his own order: Christ the firstfruits; afterward they that are Christ’s at his coming” (1 Corinthians 15:23). The word “order” is a word for a rank or a division, of the kind used of soldiers drawn up in their companies. Paul says that each one rises in his own rank, and he names two ranks: Christ, the firstfruits, and afterward “they that are Christ’s at his coming.”"),
("p", "The verse lists two. It does not list a third. Nothing is said in it of the resurrection of others, nor of an order for other companies of the dead, nor of a general resurrection. The volume follows the narrow reading of this verse that is given in the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series, Volume 4, The Resurrection Order in Scripture, where the verse is read as the order of those who are Christ’s. That is the company Paul is writing about in the chapter and the company to whom the verse is addressed. The order and company of the other resurrections of Scripture are the subject of that volume and of Study 49, The Resurrections of Scripture, and this volume does not repeat them."),
("tbl", "The order of 1 Corinthians 15:20–28, verse by verse.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("1Cor 15:20", "“is Christ risen from the dead, and become the firstfruits of them that slept”", "Christ first"),
    ("1Cor 15:23", "“Christ the firstfruits; afterward they that are Christ’s at his coming”", "Two ranks; the second is “they that are Christ’s”"),
    ("1Cor 15:24", "“Then cometh the end, when he shall have delivered up the kingdom to God, even the Father”", "A third term, “the end,” follows"),
    ("1Cor 15:25", "“he must reign, till he hath put all enemies under his feet”", "A reign with a limit"),
    ("1Cor 15:26", "“The last enemy that shall be destroyed is death”", "Death is the last of the enemies"),
    ("1Cor 15:28", "“the Son also himself be subject unto him that put all things under him, that God may be all in all”", "The purpose of the whole order"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "9.4 Verses 24–28: the end and the last enemy"),
("p", "“Then cometh the end, when he shall have delivered up the kingdom to God, even the Father; when he shall have put down all rule and all authority and power” (1 Corinthians 15:24). The next three verses give the reason. “For he must reign, till he hath put all enemies under his feet. The last enemy that shall be destroyed is death” (1 Corinthians 15:25–26). The passage looks to the end of Christ’s reign, which is a reign of subduing, and it names the last of the enemies as death."),
("p", "The statement about the enemy is the point at which the two passages meet. In Romans 5 death reigned, and the verb was used twice in the same passage (Romans 5:14, 17). In 1 Corinthians 15 death is the last enemy to be destroyed. The same enemy appears in both passages. In Romans 5 the question is how it came to reign over men, and in 1 Corinthians 15 the question is how it will be brought to an end. The two passages are in this sense two ends of one subject: death as it came and death as it ends. They are not the same sentence."),
("p", "The remainder of the paragraph speaks of the subjection of all things to the Son and of the Son’s subjection to the Father, “that God may be all in all” (1 Corinthians 15:28). The volume does not enter into the timing of these events, or the meaning of the kingdom of verse 24, or the relation of this reign to other reigns spoken of in Scripture. The chapter does not date them. It gives an order, and the volume reports the order."),
("sub", "9.5 Verses 29 to 34"),
("p", "Between the end of this paragraph and the question of verse 35 lie six verses. They include the mention of those who are “baptized for the dead” (1 Corinthians 15:29), Paul’s own danger (“I die daily,” 1 Corinthians 15:31), and the warning that “evil communications corrupt good manners” (1 Corinthians 15:33). The verse about baptism for the dead is the subject of Volume 2 of the Readers Question Series, Questions on Difficult Bible Verses, and is not treated here. These verses argue the same point from other sides, and none of them adds a statement about the two men. The volume passes on to the question of verse 35."),
]
